<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ise Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ise Province was a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on the western shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-bay">Ise Bay</a>, now forming most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mie-prefecture">Mie Prefecture</a>. It contains the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-grand-shrine">Ise Grand Shrine</a>, the most sacred site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a>, which drew millions of pilgrims in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. In that era the province was divided among several domains, including those of the Tōdō lords of Tsu and the Tokugawa lords of Kii, and it became part of Mie Prefecture in 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ise-province.jpg" alt="Ise Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Ise.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1619 the shogunate moved Tokugawa Yorinobu, the tenth son of Tokugawa Ieyasu, to Wakayama, giving him Kii province and part of <b>Ise</b> with about 555,000 koku.</blockquote><a href="/state/kii-domain">Kii Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>Todo Takatora, who had served many lords before attaching himself to Tokugawa Ieyasu, was transferred from Iyo to Iga and part of <b>Ise</b> in 1608.</blockquote><a href="/state/tsu-domain">Tsu Domain</a></li></ul></section>
